Saltatory conduction is a type of action potential conduction in which the action potential "jumps" from one node of Ranvier to the next, bypassing the internodal segments. This is common in large myelinated nerve fibres (e.g., the pyramidal spinothalamic tract). In contrast, non-saltatory conduction occurs in unmyelinated nerve fibres or myelinated fibres where the action potential propagates uniformly the entire length of the fibre. The key difference is that in saltatory conduction, the action potential "jumps" between nodes, while in non-saltatory conduction, the entire fibre is exposed to the depolarization process. This difference in conduction mechanism affects the propagation speed and energy efficiency of nerve impulses. 

\boxed{
\text{Saltatory conduction is faster and more energy-efficient than non-saltatory conduction due to the safety and speed of propagation via the nodes of Ranvier.}
}

